## Row Level Security (RLS)

### Always Enable RLS First
```sql
ALTER TABLE posts ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- Enable on EVERY table — no exceptions
```

### Policy Patterns
```sql
-- Pattern 1: User owns their own rows (most common)
CREATE POLICY "users can view own posts"
  ON posts FOR SELECT
  USING (auth.uid() = user_id);

CREATE POLICY "users can insert own posts"
  ON posts FOR INSERT
  WITH CHECK (auth.uid() = user_id);

CREATE POLICY "users can update own posts"
  ON posts FOR UPDATE
  USING (auth.uid() = user_id)
  WITH CHECK (auth.uid() = user_id);

CREATE POLICY "users can delete own posts"
  ON posts FOR DELETE
  USING (auth.uid() = user_id);

-- Pattern 2: Public read, authenticated write
CREATE POLICY "public can view published posts"
  ON posts FOR SELECT
  USING (published = true);

CREATE POLICY "authors can manage own posts"
  ON posts FOR ALL
  USING (auth.uid() = user_id);

-- Pattern 3: Role-based access via profiles table
CREATE POLICY "admins can do everything"
  ON posts FOR ALL
  USING (
    EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM user_profiles
      WHERE user_profiles.user_id = auth.uid()
        AND user_profiles.role = 'admin'
    )
  );

-- Pattern 4: Service role bypass (for Edge Functions / server)
-- Service role key bypasses RLS — never expose it client-side
```

### RLS Anti-Patterns to Avoid
```sql
-- NEVER: disabling RLS for convenience
ALTER TABLE posts DISABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY; -- NEVER do this in production

-- NEVER: overly permissive catch-all
CREATE POLICY "allow all" ON posts FOR ALL USING (true); -- NEVER

-- AVOID: N+1 in policies — use EXISTS not IN
-- BAD:
USING (user_id IN (SELECT id FROM user_profiles WHERE role = 'admin'))
-- GOOD:
USING (EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM user_profiles WHERE user_id = auth.uid() AND role = 'admin'))
```

